# Pinned `spec` revision the conformance runner validates against.
#
# The runner refuses to run if the spec checkout it is given is not at this
# commit (override with --allow-spec-drift when deliberately testing against
# unreleased vocabulary). Without a pin the suite silently tracks whatever is
# on spec `main`, so a run that passed yesterday can pass today for a
# different reason.
#
# To re-pin: check out the new spec revision, put its full SHA and its
# VOCAB_VERSIONS line below, run `make -C . conformance` (or
# `scripts/run_conformance.py`), and record the resulting counts in the PR.

# DRAFT PIN — NOT A MERGE COMMIT. This points at the head of the spec branch
# feat/clinical-1.19-medication-dates (clinical v1.19 / health.shapes.ttl v1.7),
# which is itself STACKED on the unmerged branch feat/health-2.9-coded-allergy
# and is not on `main` yet. It MUST be re-pinned to that PR's merge commit, and
# the counts re-measured, before this conformance PR leaves draft. Until then the
# pin names a commit a fresh clone of spec cannot resolve, and the previous draft
# pin it replaces was in the same position.
#
# WHAT MOVING THE PIN DOES: six fixtures are added and six verdicts change, all
# of them the new ones. Full suite 140 passed / 27 failed / 0 skipped / 167 total
# at the previous pin, 146 / 27 / 0 / 173 here. NOT ONE PRE-EXISTING FIXTURE
# CHANGES VERDICT OR REASON — compared entry by entry across both runs, 0 of 167
# differ — which is the release's "additive and strictly widening" claim measured
# rather than quoted. Constraint checks 63,543 -> 63,899.
#
# The four .WARN fixtures report NO_WARNING at the previous pin (medication 55
# checks each, condition 21 each): they are shaped, and nothing notices them,
# because none of the six constraints exists there. They pass here at 59 / 61 /
# 26 / 28. The two .VALID fixtures pass at BOTH pins, so passing is not what
# proves them; their constraint counts going 55 -> 59 and 21 -> 26 is the new
# property shapes actually evaluating.
#
# ONE MEASUREMENT WORTH RECORDING SEPARATELY, because it is evidence about the
# corpus rather than about the new fixtures. Seven pre-existing JSON fixtures
# (med-001 through med-007) already carried health:startDate, and med-007 also
# health:endDate. At this pin each of them reports one new sh:Warning (med-007
# two) from clinical:MedicationDateSpellingShape, and every one still PASSES,
# because a positive fixture is defined by the absence of sh:Violation. So the
# defect clinical v1.19 describes was already sitting in this repository's own
# corpus, unnoticed by anything, and the release is the first thing to see it.
#
# Previously pinned at the MERGE commit of the-cascade-protocol/spec PR #32 on `main`
# (core v3.8 / health v2.8 / clinical v1.17 / coverage v1.6; tags
# `vocab/core-v3.8`, `vocab/clinical-v1.17`, `vocab/coverage-v1.6` point at
# this commit; `vocab/health-v2.8` remains on the previous pin).
#
# WHAT MOVING THE PIN DID: exactly one verdict changed, and it is the one the
# previous pin's baseline predicted. clinical/status-laboratoryreport-in-progress
# .WARN.ttl -- baselined at the previous pin as a spec-owned defect (sh:node
# escalating nested sh:Warning to sh:Violation on the six document subtypes) --
# now passes: clinical v1.17 moved the two Warning bindings onto target-class
# shapes (DocumentStatusShape, DocumentReferenceStatusShape), so the warning
# fires named and nothing else does. Its KNOWN_FAILURES entry is removed in the
# same commit, per the ratchet. core v3.8 defines cascade:PatientReported, the
# provenance individual seventeen shapes already accepted; coverage v1.6 adds
# cascade:AIExtracted to InsurancePlanShape's dataProvenance set. Neither
# changes any existing fixture's verdict; the AIExtracted InsurancePlan and
# documentReferenceStatus WARN fixtures this release makes possible are tracked
# upstream rather than rushed here.
#
# Re-measured at this commit: see the PR for counts.

